>Not fair.  If a black dragon came up to you carrying $1000 and pleaded,
>would you take $500 and leave him alone?  No, you'd chop-slash, take
>the lot, then eat his corpse and tan his hide when he's dead, Fred.  

Well...depends.  First off, if I were lawful, I'd consider it,
particularly if the dragon were non-chaotic.

Second, I'd do cost-benifit analysis.  Even if the battle were, say,
75% in my favor, I might consider a 100% chance of getting 500 gp
better than a 75% chance of getting 1000 and a 25% chance of dying.
Our tough friend the gray dragon, although he might realize that the
cards were on his side, might decide that a "small" tribute would be
enough to outweigh the benifits of going through with the battle.

In the case of characters taking pleas, I'd be inclined to think that
breaking a promise not to attack would be a _very_ chaotic act.  So
chaotic characters could do it with impunity.  The same would apply to
chaotic monsters, making pleas somewhat less effective against them.

(E.g., good old gray dragon, if he were chaotic [I don't know their
alignment], might accept your tribute of your best artifact, most of
your armor, and all your nasty wands, and then happily enjoy making
mincemeat of the vastly weakened adventurer.  Ouch.)

